WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:04.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.760
MATTHEW SPALDING: Throughout
history, particular cities

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.930
have played a very
important role.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:18.170
Athens, Jerusalem, Rome,
London, Philadelphia.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:21.170
And you can kind of mark
the great stages of history

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:24.230
and the great developments of
human liberty by those places.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:31.010
The American place
that was created

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:35.100
as a result of what happened in
Philadelphia is Washington, DC.

00:00:41.561 --> 00:00:44.500
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY: Washington,
DC is where the action is.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.342
If you want to affect
our federal government,

00:00:47.342 --> 00:00:48.550
this is where you have to be.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.730
And there's so many
opportunities to make a change.

00:00:54.376 --> 00:00:57.520
PATRICK BRENNAN: I moved to
Washington to change things,

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.715
to improve and advance
American government.

00:00:59.715 --> 00:01:01.670
JAMIE DANGERS: This is
where laws are made.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.719
This is where policy happens.

00:01:06.812 --> 00:01:08.270
MATTHEW SPALDING:
It's a place that

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:13.220
represents the idea of
America and a capital of one

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:18.100
of the greatest countries
in the world and in history.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.410
And this is the place
to change the country.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:23.510
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:27.460
Graduate programs in Washington,
DC are about a dime a dozen.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:28.940
There are lots of them.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:32.440
And most of them teach you
to accommodate yourself

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:37.790
to the status quo, how
Washington works today.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.920
We want to do
something different.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.610
We want to teach
you how to think

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:44.820
so you know those
first principles,

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.420
and you're able to
have good judgment,

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:49.623
and deliberate, and take action.

00:01:49.623 --> 00:01:51.040
JAMIE DANGERS:
These days, culture

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.040
tells us to react, to respond.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:02:00.340
And it's all based on
feeling, based on hype.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:05.200
I want the change I am
part of to be substantive

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.430
and to be thoughtful, and
there has to be a grounding.

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:14.680
MATTHEW SPALDING: What's missing
in a lot of politics today

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.715
is we tend to have
one or the other.

00:02:17.715 --> 00:02:19.090
We have a lot of
people that want

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.467
to talk about first principles.

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:22.300
And, of course, we have
a lot of people that

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.340
want to focus just on action.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:30.180
It's connecting the two that's
the virtue of statesmanship.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.418
TOM COTTON: Education
is about more than just

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:33.460
turning out good workers.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:36.230
It should be about turning
out good citizens as well,

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:37.990
the next generation
of Americans who

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.810
understand the timeless
principles of our government.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:42.970
And unfortunately, too
many schools and especially

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:45.950
universities not only
fail in that mission,

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:47.763
but they actively
repudiate that mission.

00:02:47.763 --> 00:02:49.930
MOLLIE HEMINGWAY: Young
people who come to this town

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:53.040
don't have that
founding understanding

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.264
of how government should be
and how it will best operate.

00:02:57.264 --> 00:03:01.340
JAMIE DANGERS: So I'm excited
about learning the larger

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:05.480
story, and how to incorporate
the wisdom of people

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:11.390
who came before us into a very
fraught culture right now.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.330
LARRY ARNN: This
graduate school was

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:19.040
inspired by George
Washington to teach

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.070
these things, the large
things that a statesman would

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:24.370
need to know to govern
this country well.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.640
MATTHEW SPALDING: We're going
to look at core documents,

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.030
original documents.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.570
We're going to
study the writings

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:34.155
and thoughts of great
statesmen, great books.

00:03:34.155 --> 00:03:35.530
LARRY ARNN: And
it will mostly be

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:38.110
taken by people who
work in the government,

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:40.750
and they will read old books.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:44.320
They'll start with Plato
and Aristotle, Herodotus,

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.120
and Thucydides.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:48.310
And they'll learn
what politics is,

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:52.380
and then they'll
learn what America is.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:55.510
JAMIE DANGERS: I think
this program is unique

00:03:55.510 --> 00:04:01.120
because it marries political
thought, and philosophy,

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.990
and wisdom from the
ancients with governance

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:06.655
and statesmanship
where we are now.

00:04:06.655 --> 00:04:08.030
PATRICK BRENNAN:
There's a reason

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.690
why people study old texts.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:13.480
They've proven their value,
but they're also rigorous.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.580
And so you really
can't dumb them down.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:17.950
MATTHEW SPALDING: We
want to study that now

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:19.149
not because it's the past.

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:23.800
We want to study it because
the past will help us now

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:24.640
to shape the future.

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:29.320
What the Van Andel Graduate
School of Government

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:33.310
wants to do is help
you see the North Star,

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:35.882
because if you
don't have that, why

00:04:35.882 --> 00:04:37.090
are you involved in politics?

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:50.430
MATTHEW MEHAN: What excites
me most about teaching here I

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:53.880
think would have to be
the opportunity what

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:57.870
Socrates called protreptic,
the turning of souls.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:00.900
I really think that this
school of government,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:05.310
and this curriculum, and
these teachers, this place,

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.460
this town, you can
actually turn people

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:12.300
towards a life of deep
and prudent service

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.883
if you give them
the right principles

00:05:13.883 --> 00:05:15.300
and you give them
the right tools.

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:27.170
MATTHEW SPALDING: We are in
many ways in a crisis today

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:29.270
about who should govern.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:31.100
Are we still a
self-governing people?

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:34.910
The role of Congress versus the
president, the Supreme Court,

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:38.720
consent, these are
the core questions

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.800
that actually go back to
the very founding itself.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:44.870
And as an extension of
our teaching mission,

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:47.060
it's only natural
that we are going

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:51.020
to teach those ideas which we've
taught from the very beginning

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:56.900
here in this city to all those
who wish to perpetuate liberty

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:57.740
in today's world.

00:06:01.086 --> 00:06:03.750
LARRY ARNN: We want
to live freely.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:05.400
That's not so easy
these days, right?

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:06.377
We do huge things.

00:06:06.377 --> 00:06:08.210
We don't take any money
from the government.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:08.710
Why?

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:12.420
Because we think it's
of eternal value,

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:14.860
and we think we
have a right to it,

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:18.010
and so we need a
freedom to let us do it.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:25.600
MATTHEW SPALDING: There will
be many difficult and prudent

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.340
decisions required
to recover a stronger

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:32.485
sense of self-government,
a limited government,

00:06:32.485 --> 00:06:34.540
a constitutional government.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:37.190
It's going to take some time.

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:39.700
But we need to
prepare now to shape

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:41.870
those people who will do that.

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:42.910
It's not too late.

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:44.920
TOM COTTON: I want to
see the next generation

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:47.350
of American statesmen prepared
to face those challenges,

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:48.730
should they come.

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:51.400
And the lessons they
learn are being taken out

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.650
of the classroom at
Hillsdale and being

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:55.150
taken into the
halls of Congress,

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.750
and into the administration,
and into the courts.

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:03.210
MATTHEW SPALDING:
The greatest thing

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:05.500
that the Van Andel Graduate
School of Government

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:08.470
in Washington, DC
can accomplish is

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:15.190
to form a core of individuals
who have the knowledge,

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:18.925
and the determination, the
good sense, and the prudence,

00:07:18.925 --> 00:07:22.940
of going out into the
world in order to turn it,

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:26.590
turn it back to its
principled course of the good

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:29.430
of preserving self-government.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:33.960
Forming those kinds of
individuals, statesmen,

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:38.870
and being able to teach
them, and see them formed

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:43.410
would be the greatest
accomplishment,

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:45.640
and for the good of our country.

